            "title": "Colonial Stages of British Exploitation",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. R.P. Dutt's Framework of Colonial Exploitation</h3>\n<p>In his seminal work <em>India Today (1940)</em>, Marxist political economist <strong>Rajani Palme Dutt</strong> identified three successive, evolutionary stages of British economic exploitation in India, each corresponding to a specific phase of British capitalist development:</p>\n\n<h3>2. Stage 1: The Period of Merchant Capital / Mercantilism (1757–1813)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Economic Mechanism:</strong> Following the Battle of Plassey (1757) and the Grant of Diwani (1765), the East India Company acquired sovereign taxation rights. Prior to 1757, the Company had to import millions of pounds worth of gold and silver bullion from Britain to buy Indian silk, cotton, and spices. Post-1765, the Company used surplus land revenues (termed <em>'Investments'</em>) to purchase Indian goods for export to Europe and China.</li>\n  <li><strong>Characteristics:</strong> Direct plunder, extortion, monopolistic control over inland trade (salt, betel nut, tobacco), and coercion of Indian weavers and artisans. There was no attempt to alter traditional Indian village social structures, only to milk the existing agrarian revenue.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Stage 2: Industrial Capitalism &amp; Free Trade (1813–1858)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Economic Mechanism:</strong> The Industrial Revolution transformed Britain into the 'Workshop of the World'. British industrialists demanded raw materials for their mechanized factories and vast markets for their mass-produced manufactured textiles.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Shift:</strong> The <strong>Charter Act of 1813</strong> ended the Company's trade monopoly. One-way free trade was imposed: British manufactured goods entered India with negligible tariffs (2–3%), while Indian textiles in Britain were crushed under protective duties of 70–80%.</li>\n  <li><strong>Transformation of India:</strong> India's traditional role as the world's premier exporter of finished textiles was completely reversed: India was subordinated into an exporter of agricultural raw materials (raw cotton, raw jute, indigo, opium, oilseeds) and an importer of British factory cloth.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. Stage 3: Financial Capitalism / Finance Imperialism (Post-1858)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Economic Mechanism:</strong> Vast accumulation of surplus capital in Britain sought high-yield, secure overseas investment. Post-1858, British finance capital poured into India, concentrated in:\n    <ol>\n      <li><strong>Guaranteed Railways:</strong> Private British railway companies were guaranteed a 5% risk-free return paid from the Indian treasury.</li>\n      <li><strong>Export Plantations:</strong> British-owned tea, coffee, rubber, and indigo estates.</li>\n      <li><strong>Extractive Industries &amp; Banking:</strong> Coal mining, jute mills, shipping monopolies, and the Managing Agency System (Andrew Yule, Bird &amp; Co).</li>\n      <li><strong>Public Debt:</strong> Government of India loans raised on the London money market to finance imperial wars in Afghanistan, Burma, and China.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n",
