            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Pre-Colonial Land Tenures and Social Relations</h3>\n<p>In traditional pre-colonial India, land was not a commodity to be bought, sold, or mortgaged in a commercial market. The socio-economic fabric rested on the self-sufficient <strong>village community</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>King's Share:</strong> The monarch or state was entitled only to a share of the gross agricultural yield (termed <em>Bhaga</em> in Hindu law and <em>Kharaj</em> in Islamic law), traditionally varying from one-sixth to one-third, though Mughal emperors like Akbar (Zabti system) and Aurangzeb extracted up to one-half.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hereditary Occupancy:</strong> The cultivator (<em>Khudkasht ryot</em>) possessed hereditary, inalienable rights to cultivate the soil. So long as he rendered the customary state share, no king or landlord could legally evict him.</li>\n  <li><strong>Role of Intermediaries:</strong> Pre-colonial <strong>Zamindars</strong>, <em>Chaudhuris</em>, and <em>Talukdars</em> were tax collectors and revenue managers, not private capitalist owners of the soil. They held a right to collect revenue (<em>Malikana</em>), but possessed no proprietary right to evict peasants or sell village common lands.</li>\n  <li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> In years of drought, floods, or pestilence, the state share automatically declined, and revenue was routinely suspended or remitted.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Colonial Dilemma: The Need for Maximized, Fixed Cash Revenue</h3>\n<p>Following the Grant of Diwani in 1765, the East India Company encountered three pressing fiscal necessities:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Funding Corporate 'Investments':</strong> Purchasing Indian textiles, spices, and Chinese tea for shipment to London without importing British silver.</li>\n  <li><strong>Financing Imperial Wars:</strong> Raising and maintaining huge sepoy armies to fight Mysore, the Marathas, and the French.</li>\n  <li><strong>Administrative Overhead:</strong> Paying lavish salaries and pensions to British civil servants.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Land revenue was the Company's single greatest source of income (accounting for over 80% of total state revenue). The Company demanded that revenue be paid <strong>in hard cash</strong>, at <strong>fixed calendar deadlines</strong>, regardless of seasonal harvest fluctuations.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Early British Disasters: Farming Systems (1772–1789)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Quinquennial Settlement (1772):</strong> Warren Hastings introduced a five-year revenue farming system, auctioning the right to collect revenue to the highest bidding speculative contractor (<em>Ijaradar</em>). Traditional zamindars were pushed out by urban money merchants who extorted the peasantry ruthlessly to recover their bid money.</li>\n  <li><strong>Annual Settlements (1777):</strong> When the 5-year experiment caused mass desertion of lands and revenue defaults, Hastings reverted to annual leases, which heightened instability and prevented any capital investment in agriculture.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. The Great Property Revolution</h3>\n<p>Recognizing that chaotic auction farming was destroying Bengal's agricultural base, British administrators determined to establish regular, structured land tenure systems. In doing so, the British introduced a fundamental revolution: <strong>transforming land into private, alienable, mortgageable property</strong>. If the state revenue was unpaid, the land was seized and sold at public auction. This legal innovation gave birth to three giant land revenue systems across British India:</p>\n<table class=\"table-module\">\n  <tr><th>System</th><th>Architect</th><th>Regions</th><th>Total Area of British India</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Permanent Settlement (Zamindari)</strong></td><td>Lord Cornwallis (1793)</td><td>Bengal, Bihar, Orissa, Varanasi, Northern Circars</td><td><strong>19%</strong></td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Ryotwari System</strong></td><td>Alexander Read &amp; Thomas Munro (1820)</td><td>Madras, Bombay, Assam, Coorg</td><td><strong>51%</strong></td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Mahalwari System</strong></td><td>Holt Mackenzie &amp; R.M. Bird (1822/1833)</td><td>Gangetic Valley, NWFP, Central Provinces, Punjab</td><td><strong>30%</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n",
