            "title": "The Permanent Settlement of Bengal (1793)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Genesis of the Permanent Settlement</h3>\n<p>Between 1786 and 1793, Lord Cornwallis debated the future land revenue structure with two leading experts: <strong>Sir John Shore</strong> (who advocated a 10-year settlement with zamindars that could be revised based on future surveys) and <strong>James Grant</strong> (who argued that all land belonged to the sovereign and zamindars were mere tax agents). Cornwallis overruled Shore's caution: he introduced the <strong>Decennial Settlement (1789–1790)</strong>, and on <strong>May 1, 1793</strong>, with the approval of Prime Minister Pitt, proclaimed it <strong>Permanent in Perpetuity (Istamrari Bandobast)</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Key Features of the Permanent Settlement</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Zamindars as Absolute Owners:</strong> The traditional zamindars and taluqdars were elevated into legal owners of the soil. Their proprietary right was made hereditary, transferable, and saleable.</li>\n  <li><strong>Perpetual Fixation of State Demand:</strong> The revenue assessment was fixed forever. The government solemnly pledged that it would never increase its demand on the zamindars under any circumstances, regardless of future agricultural expansion or price inflation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Revenue Apportionment:</strong> Of the total rental collected from the peasants:\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>10/11th (approx. 89%)</strong> was remitted to the East India Company's treasury.</li>\n      <li><strong>1/11th (approx. 11%)</strong> was retained by the zamindar as his remuneration.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Crushing Initial Assessment:</strong> The revenue fixed was not a modest sum: it was calculated at the extraordinarily oppressive peak figure of <strong>₹2.68 crore</strong> (nearly double the revenue collected during the late Mughal era).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Motives of the British</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Financial Security:</strong> Guaranteed a fixed, predictable revenue every year on precise dates, insulating the Company from harvest failures and seasonal market slumps.</li>\n  <li><strong>Political Loyalty:</strong> Created a staunchly loyal class of wealthy landed aristocrats who had a vested interest in the survival of British rule. During the 1857 Revolt, the zamindars of Bengal remained completely loyal to the British Crown.</li>\n  <li><strong>Administrative Simplicity:</strong> Relieved the Company of the enormous administrative burden of conducting detailed land surveys and dealing with millions of individual peasant cultivators.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ideological Fantasy:</strong> Cornwallis, himself an English aristocrat, naively imagined that Indian zamindars would transform into enlightened British-style country squires (improving landlords) who would invest capital in drainage, irrigation, and modern agricultural technology.</li>\n</ul>\n",
