            "title": "The Ryotwari System (Read & Munro)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Origins &amp; Rejection of the Zamindari System in the South</h3>\n<p>Following the Third and Fourth Anglo-Mysore Wars (1792, 1799), the East India Company acquired extensive territories in Southern India (Baramahal, Dindigul, Malabar, Coimbatore). When the British attempted to introduce Cornwallis's Permanent Settlement, it collapsed because Southern India lacked a traditional class of large hereditary zamindars like Bengal. Local chiefs (<em>Poligars</em>) were rebellious military barons rather than peaceful revenue collectors.</p>\n<p>Two visionary Scottish administrators—<strong>Captain Alexander Read</strong> and his assistant <strong>Thomas Munro</strong>—devised an alternative system in the <strong>Baramahal district in 1792</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Thomas Munro &amp; Systematization (1820)</h3>\n<p>When <strong>Sir Thomas Munro</strong> became Governor of Madras in <strong>1820</strong>, he officially abolished the experimental zamindari tenures in the south and introduced the <strong>Ryotwari System</strong> across the entire <strong>Madras Presidency</strong>. It was subsequently extended to:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Bombay Presidency:</strong> Introduced by Governor <strong>Mountstuart Elphinstone</strong>, and later scientifically surveyed and revised by <strong>Pringle (1824–1828)</strong> and the <strong>Wingate-Goldsmid Survey (1835)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Other Areas:</strong> Parts of <strong>Assam</strong> and the small province of <strong>Coorg</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Overall, Ryotwari covered <strong>51% of the total territory of British India</strong>—the largest land system in the empire.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Essential Mechanics of the Ryotwari System</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Direct State-Peasant Nexus:</strong> The government established direct legal relations with the <strong>Ryot (individual cultivator)</strong>. There were no intermediary zamindars, taluqdars, or revenue farmers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Peasant Ownership:</strong> The ryot was recognized as the legal proprietor of his land. He had the right to cultivate, sell, transfer, or mortgage his plot so long as he regularly paid the state revenue.</li>\n  <li><strong>Periodic Reassessment:</strong> The revenue was <strong>not permanent</strong>. It was fixed for a temporary period of <strong>20 to 30 years</strong>, after which the land was resurveyed and the tax was raised by the state.</li>\n  <li><strong>Individual Assessment based on Soil Productivity:</strong> Every field was surveyed, measured, mapped, and classified according to soil fertility, irrigation access, and estimated crop yield.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>4. The Theory vs. Brutal Reality</h3>\n<p>While British administrators hailed Ryotwari as an egalitarian system protecting the peasant from feudal landlords, in actual practice it proved equally crushing:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Excessive Initial Assessment:</strong> Munro initially fixed the state demand at an exorbitant rate of <strong>nearly half (45% to 55%) of the gross produce</strong>, leaving the ryot with barely enough food for subsistence.</li>\n  <li><strong>The State as Giant Landlord:</strong> The colonial government became an all-powerful, unaccountable super-zamindar. If the ryot failed to pay, his land was confiscated by the Collector.</li>\n  <li><strong>Coercion:</strong> Tax was collected with extreme brutality by subordinate Indian tahsildars (investigated by the famous <strong>Madras Torture Commission of 1855</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Ryotwari System was first introduced on an experimental basis by Captain Alexander Read in Baramahal district (Tamil Nadu) in 1792.",
                "Sir Thomas Munro (Governor of Madras 1820–1827) extended and systematized the Ryotwari settlement across the entire Madras Presidency in 1820.",
                "Concept: Direct settlement between the colonial state and the individual peasant cultivator ('Ryot'), with NO intermediary zamindars or landlords.",
                "Geographical Coverage: Applied across 51% of British India, encompassing Madras Presidency, Bombay Presidency (extended by Mountstuart Elphinstone and Wingate/Goldsmid), parts of Assam, and Coorg.",
                "Assessment Rate: Unlike the Permanent Settlement, revenue was NOT fixed permanently; it was periodically reassessed every 20 to 30 years, and fixed at an oppressive rate of 45% to 55% of gross agricultural produce."
            ],
