            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Indigo Exploitation System</h3>\n<p>Indigo (<em>neel</em>), the prized natural blue dye, became one of the earliest and most brutally enforced commercial crops in colonial India:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Nij and Ryoti Cultivation:</strong> Planters used two methods:\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Nij-abad:</em> The planter cultivated indigo on land he directly controlled using hired bullocks and laborers. This proved expensive and inefficient.</li>\n      <li><em>Ryoti System:</em> Far more common. Planters forced the <em>ryot</em> to sign a contract and accept an advance (<em>dadan</em>). The peasant was required to grow indigo on at least 25% of his best fertile land (where rice was normally grown).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Coercion and Terror:</strong> European planters maintained private armies of club-wielding thugs (<em>lathiyals</em>). Peasants who refused to sow indigo had their cattle impounded, huts burned, and family members assaulted. When the crop was harvested, planters paid arbitrary, below-market prices and fraudulently weighed the produce.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Tinkathia System of Champaran:</strong> In Bihar, planters instituted the <strong>Tinkathia</strong> system, legally compelling tenant farmers to cultivate indigo on <strong>3/20th</strong> (3 <em>kathas</em> out of every <em>bigha</em> of 20 <em>kathas</em>) of their best arable land, setting the stage for Mahatma Gandhi's historic satyagraha in 1917.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The British Opium Monopoly &amp; Plantation Capitalism</h3>\n<p>The colonial state's involvement in plantation and narcotic cash crops was direct, monopolistic, and draconian:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Opium Monopoly:</strong> The cultivation of the opium poppy was an absolute government monopoly managed by two state agencies: the <strong>Patna Agency</strong> (Bihar) and the <strong>Banaras Agency</strong> (UP). Peasants were forced to take cash advances and deliver raw opium paste exclusively to state factories at depressed prices. The refined opium was auctioned in Calcutta and smuggled into China, sparking the Anglo-Chinese Opium Wars (1839–1842 and 1856–1860).</li>\n  <li><strong>Tea and Coffee Plantations:</strong> Following the discovery of indigenous tea plants in Assam in the 1820s (by Robert Bruce), European planters acquired vast tracts of fertile forest land under the highly favorable <em>Waste Land Rules</em> at nominal prices.</li>\n  <li><strong>Indentured Labor &amp; Penal Laws:</strong> Because local Assamese villagers refused to work under degrading plantation conditions, British planters recruited impoverished tribal laborers from Chota Nagpur and the Santhal Parganas under deceptive contracts (the <em>Arkatti</em> system). The colonial government passed the <strong>Workmen's Breach of Contract Act of 1859</strong> and the <strong>Inland Emigration Acts</strong>, which criminalized laborers attempting to leave plantations, giving planters the legal power to arrest, flog, and jail runaway coolies.</li>\n</ol>\n",
