            "title": "Deindustrialization & Ruin of Indian Handicrafts",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Concept of Deindustrialization</h3>\n<p>In the 18th century, India enjoyed a pre-eminent position in global manufacturing. Renowned economic historian <strong>Paul Bairoch</strong> estimated that in 1750, India accounted for almost <strong>25% of global industrial output</strong>. India's fine cotton textiles, silks, woolens, metallurgy (such as Wootz steel), and shipbuilding were exported across Asia, Africa, and Europe.</p>\n<p>By the mid-19th century, this vibrant artisan sector was systematically crushed through a process termed <strong>Deindustrialization</strong>. Unlike Britain, where displaced agricultural workers were absorbed into booming urban factories, in India the destruction of traditional crafts was not accompanied by modern industrial growth.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Mechanisms Behind the Destruction of Traditional Crafts</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Collapse of Royal Patronage:</strong> Indian royal courts and aristocratic nobles were the primary patrons of luxury handicrafts (fine muslins, embroidered shawls, inlay work, arms, and jewelry). As Dalhousie and Wellesley annexed Indian princely states, these traditional patrons vanished overnight.</li>\n  <li><strong>One-Way Free Trade &amp; Tariff Manipulation:</strong> While the British Parliament placed prohibitively high import tariffs (ranging from 70% to 80%) on Indian handloom textiles entering Britain to protect its infant domestic mills, British manufactured goods entered India practically duty-free (with nominal 2.5% to 3.5% customs duties).</li>\n  <li><strong>The Machine Influx:</strong> Steam-powered mechanization in Lancashire and Manchester produced cotton yarn and cloth at a fraction of the cost of Indian hand-spun yarn. Inexpensive, standardized British cloth flooded Indian bazaars via new railway lines.</li>\n  <li><strong>Coercion of Weavers:</strong> Under the <em>Gomastha</em> system, Company agents coerced weavers into accepting starvation wages and restricted them from selling their fabrics to other European traders or Indian merchants.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Catastrophic Human Impact</h3>\n<p>The collapse of artisanal crafts created unprecedented distress. Millions of weavers, spinners, dyers, blacksmiths, and metalworkers lost their livelihood. Having no factories to turn to, these millions of displaced artisans had no alternative but to crowd into agricultural villages as impoverished, landless farm laborers. This led to the <strong>ruralization of India</strong> and an unsustainable pressure on arable land.</p>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Deindustrialization refers to the systematic destruction and decline of India's traditional handicraft and artisan manufacturing industries without the emergence of modern mechanized industries to replace them.",
                "Prior to 1750, India was the manufacturing workshop of the world, supplying roughly 25% of global industrial output, especially world-famous cotton textiles (Muslin of Dacca, Calico of Calicut, Chintz of Machilipatnam).",
                "Causes of ruin: Loss of royal court patronage due to the annexation of princely states, unfair one-way tariff policies favoring British goods, influx of cheap machine-made textiles from Manchester, and Company coercion of weavers.",
                "The Charter Act of 1813 ended the Company's trade monopoly with India, throwing open Indian markets to British industrial goods.",
                "In 1834–35, Governor-General Lord William Bentinck reported: 'The misery hardly finds a parallel in the history of commerce. The bones of the cotton-weavers are bleaching the plains of India.'",
                "Displaced urban artisans migrated en masse to rural villages, creating immense overcrowding on agriculture and turning India into an agrarian appendage of industrial Britain."
            ],
