            "title": "Nationalist Economic Critique (R.C. Dutt, G.V. Joshi, G.K. Gokhale)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. R.C. Dutt and 'The Economic History of India'</h3>\n<p>While Dadabhai Naoroji highlighted the external drain of capital, <strong>Romesh Chunder Dutt</strong> (1848–1909), a distinguished former Indian Civil Service (ICS) officer and President of the 1899 Lucknow INC session, analyzed the internal agrarian and industrial mechanisms of colonial exploitation in his monumental two-volume study:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>The Economic History of India under Early British Rule</em> (1902)</li>\n  <li><em>The Economic History of India in the Victorian Age</em> (1904)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dutt meticulously documented how the British administration's exorbitant land tax destroyed peasant reserves, noting: <em>'If India is poor today, it is through the operation of economic causes... the taxation was heavy, the revenue system was inelastic, and the expenditure was lavish.'</em> He urged the colonial government to extend the Permanent Settlement principles or cap land revenue at one-fifth of gross produce.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Ranade, Joshi &amp; The Poona School of Economics</h3>\n<p>In Western India, the <strong>Poona Sarvajanik Sabha</strong> became an intellectual powerhouse of economic analysis:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Justice Mahadev Govind Ranade (1842–1901):</strong> Often called the 'Father of Indian National Economics'. In his 1892 lecture on <em>'Indian Economics'</em>, Ranade rejected classical British laissez-faire free-market dogmas (Adam Smith and Ricardo) as unsuitable for colonial India. He argued that the state must play an active developmental role in establishing modern industries, advancing agricultural credit, and building technical colleges.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ganesh Vyankatesh Joshi (G.V. Joshi, 1851–1911):</strong> Known affectionately as <em>'Sarvajanik Kaka'</em>, Joshi was an unparalleled statistical genius who prepared rigorous data sheets exposing railway overspending, exorbitant salt taxes, and agrarian impoverishment. Both Ranade and Gokhale drew heavily on Joshi's statistical compilations.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. G.K. Gokhale and Parliamentary Financial Warfare</h3>\n<p><strong>Gopal Krishna Gokhale</strong> (1866–1915) mastered the art of budgetary critique in the <strong>Imperial Legislative Council</strong> between 1902 and 1912. Armed with ironclad statistics, Gokhale relentlessly attacked:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The crushing, regressive <strong>Salt Tax</strong> that penalized the poorest peasants.</li>\n  <li>The massive share of Indian revenues (over 40% to 50%) squandered on British military expeditions beyond India's borders.</li>\n  <li>The total neglect of primary education, sanitation, and agricultural irrigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Together with <strong>Dinshaw Edulji Wacha</strong>, Gokhale and Naoroji presented comprehensive indictments before the Welby Commission, shattering the illusion of British fiscal benevolence.</p>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Romesh Chunder Dutt (R.C. Dutt), a retired Indian ICS officer and economic historian, authored the classic two-volume work: 'The Economic History of India' (1902 & 1904).",
                "R.C. Dutt focused on excessive and rigid land revenue assessments as the primary cause of rural poverty and recurrent famines.",
                "Justice Mahadev Govind Ranade emphasized that India's fundamental economic malady was its excessive reliance on agriculture and total lack of modern manufacturing industries.",
                "G.V. Joshi (Sarvajanik Kaka) of the Poona Sarvajanik Sabha conducted pioneering statistical studies on colonial finances, rural debt, and public works extravagance.",
                "Gopal Krishna Gokhale used the Imperial Legislative Council as a platform to challenge colonial budgets, excessive military spending, and the salt tax.",
                "Dinshaw Wacha, along with Naoroji, presented devastating evidence before the Welby Commission (1895) on military expenditure and financial mismanagement.",
                "The nationalist economic critique dismantled the ideological moral justification of British rule, providing the intellectual foundation for the freedom struggle."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "R.C. Dutt's 'The Economic History of India' remains the foundational masterwork of Indian economic historiography.",
                "Nationalists united in demanding: reduction of land revenue, abolition of the salt tax, protective tariffs for Indian industries, and reduction of crushing military budgets."
            ],
