            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Famines: The Human Cost of Colonial Extraction</h3>\n<p>Before British rule, famines were localized phenomena caused by extreme weather; traditional rulers responded by opening royal granaries, suspending taxes, and providing food relief. Under British colonial rule, famines became structural, widespread, and vastly more lethal. Historian <strong>William Digby</strong> calculated that over <strong>28 million Indians perished in famines</strong> between 1854 and 1901 alone.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Chronology of Major Colonial Famines</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Great Bengal Famine of 1770:</strong> The first great colonial catastrophe occurred just five years after the Grant of Diwani. A failure of rains was turned into an apocalypse by the East India Company's ruthless tax collection and grain hoarding. Over <strong>10 million people (one-third of Bengal's entire population)</strong> starved to death. Shockingly, the Company collected more land revenue in 1771 than in 1768!</li>\n  <li><strong>The Orissa Famine of 1866:</strong> When the monsoon failed in Orissa, colonial authorities refused to import grain, blindly believing in laissez-faire dogmas. While over a million people perished (one-third of Orissa's population), the government exported over 200 million pounds of rice from Bengal to Britain. This disaster prompted the appointment of the <em>George Campbell Commission</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Great Famine of 1876–1878:</strong> Covering over 200,000 square miles across Madras, Mysore, Bombay, and Hyderabad, this famine claimed an estimated <strong>6 to 8 million lives</strong>. At the height of the starvation:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Viceroy <strong>Lord Lytton</strong> staged the lavish, multimillion-rupee <strong>Imperial Delhi Durbar of 1877</strong> to proclaim Queen Victoria 'Empress of India'.</li>\n      <li>Record shipments of Indian wheat were exported to England.</li>\n      <li>Sir Richard Temple imposed a starvation labor ration (the infamous 'Temple Wage') providing less energy than the diet later given at Nazi concentration camps.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The Famines of 1896–97 and 1899–1900:</strong> Devastated the Central Provinces, United Provinces, and Western India. Bapu and Tilak organized relief, while British authorities imposed harsh labor tests under the Famine Codes.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Bengal Famine of 1943:</strong> During World War II, between <strong>1.5 and 3 million people died</strong> in Bengal. Nobel laureate <strong>Amartya Sen</strong> demonstrated that food availability was only marginally lower than average; the famine was caused by:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Winston Churchill's British war cabinet hoarding grain stockpiles in the Mediterranean.</li>\n      <li>The colonial 'Boat Denial Policy' and 'Rice Denial Policy' destroying inland transport in coastal Bengal to counter a potential Japanese invasion.</li>\n      <li>Uncontrolled wartime inflation driving grain prices completely beyond the purchasing capacity of landless laborers and rural artisans.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n",
