            "title": "Emergence of Modern Indian Industries (Cotton, Jute, Coal, TISCO)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Dawn of Modern Factory Industry</h3>\n<p>Modern machine-based industrialization began in India in the 1850s. However, this growth was slow, lopsided, and marked by intense racial-colonial competition between British managing agencies and emerging indigenous Indian entrepreneurs:</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Modern Cotton Textile Industry</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>First Mill:</strong> In 1854, <strong>Cowasji Nanabhoy Davar</strong>, a pioneering Parsi merchant, established the <em>Bombay Spinning and Weaving Company</em> in Bombay, which commenced production in 1856.</li>\n  <li><strong>Growth Centers:</strong> Bombay became the 'Cottonopolis' of India due to its proximity to raw cotton fields of the Deccan, natural port facilities, humid coastal climate, and abundant supply of cheap labor from the Konkan.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ahmedabad:</strong> In 1861, <strong>Ranchhodlal Chhotalal</strong> founded the first cotton mill in Ahmedabad, which soon earned the title of the <em>'Manchester of India'</em>. By 1914, India had over 270 cotton mills, overwhelmingly owned and managed by indigenous Indian capital.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. The Jute and Coal Industries</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Jute Mills of Bengal:</strong> In 1855, an Englishman named <strong>George Acland</strong> established India's first machine-driven jute spinning mill at <strong>Rishra</strong> near Serampore on the Hooghly river. Unlike cotton, the jute industry was dominated by Scottish managing agencies (centered around Dundee and Calcutta) and backed by the powerful <em>Indian Jute Mills Association (IJMA)</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Coal Mining:</strong> First opened in 1774 at Raniganj, large-scale commercial coal extraction surged after 1854 with the expansion of the East Indian Railway. The <strong>Raniganj and Jharia</strong> coalfields in Bengal and Bihar provided over 85% of India's coal output, predominantly controlled by British firms (such as Andrew Yule and Bird &amp; Co.).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>4. Jamsetji Tata &amp; TISCO: The Heavy Industry Breakthrough</h3>\n<p>Because the British colonial administration refused to build heavy machine-tool or capital goods industries, India remained dependent on Britain for machinery. <strong>Jamsetji Nusserwanji Tata</strong> (1839–1904) recognized that true national independence required heavy metallurgy, hydroelectric power, and scientific education:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>His vision was realized by his sons, Sir Dorabji Tata and Sir Ratan Tata, who founded the <strong>Tata Iron and Steel Company (TISCO)</strong> in <strong>1907</strong> at Sakchi (later renamed <strong>Jamshedpur</strong>), situated near rich iron ore deposits in Mayurbhanj and coal in Jharia.</li>\n  <li>TISCO began commercial production of pig iron in 1911 and steel ingots in 1912. It was funded entirely by Indian capital raised from thousands of ordinary citizens, standing as a monumental monument to national self-reliance (<em>Swadeshi</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Modern machine-based factory industry emerged in India in the second half of the 19th century, characterized by an unequal racial division: European capital dominated plantations, jute, coal, and shipping, while Indian capital broke into cotton textiles.",
                "The Cotton Textile Industry: The first modern Indian cotton textile mill was established in 1854 at Bombay by Cowasji Nanabhoy Davar (the Bombay Spinning and Weaving Company).",
                "Bombay, and later Ahmedabad ('Manchester of India'), became the stronghold of indigenous Indian textile entrepreneurship led by Parsi, Gujarati, and Jain merchants.",
                "The Jute Industry: The first modern jute mill was set up in 1855 at Rishra near Calcutta by George Acland; the jute industry remained an almost exclusive monopoly of British/Scottish capital organized under the Indian Jute Mills Association (IJMA).",
                "Coal Mining: Centered in the Raniganj and Jharia coalfields (Bengal and Bihar), driven by the demand of the expanding railway network.",
                "The Iron & Steel Industry: Jamsetji Nusserwanji Tata founded the Tata Iron and Steel Company (TISCO) in 1907 at Sakchi (Jamshedpur), which began producing pig iron in 1911 and steel in 1912—the greatest triumph of indigenous Indian heavy industrial enterprise.",
                "Colonial Obstacles: Indian industries faced severe handicaps: lack of heavy capital goods manufacturing, discriminatory railway freights, hostile British managing agencies, and zero state protective tariffs."
            ],
